@capability-accessibility
@oracle-std-sort-and-serde-json
Feature: Visual windows are operable and understandable without sight or pointer
  Announcements are bilingual; focus runs outliner, viewport, properties, checks, diagrams and is never trapped; every function has a documented shortcut.

  Further specification assertions, asserted by the Rust unit tests of the crate and not by the platform adapters:
  - Evaluation results are announced: Given an evaluation with passing, warning and failing checks When the evaluation announcement is built Then it names counts and the highest utilisation in English and German, and is assertive when anything fails
  - Utilisation changes are announced sparingly: Given utilisation before and after an edit When changes are compared Then only status changes and moves of at least 5 percentage points are announced and new failures interrupt
  - Announcements are coalesced and capped: Given several pending announcements for the same entity When the live region is drained with a limit Then the latest per entity wins, assertive comes first and the overflow is summarised
  - Focus order and no trap: Given any device class When Tab and Shift+Tab are pressed repeatedly Then focus visits every region once and then leaves the visual windows
  - Shortcuts: Given the documented shortcut table Then no chord is used twice, every label exists in English and German, every region has a focus shortcut and arrow keys orbit
  - Preferences choose the theme variant: Given forced colours, more contrast, dark scheme or reduced motion Then forced colours and more contrast select high contrast, dark selects dark and reduced motion disables animations
  - Shortcuts are scoped, so handles nudge and the viewport orbits on the same arrow keys: Given the shortcut table with global, viewport and handle scopes Then with a handle focused the arrows nudge it, Shift takes a large step and Alt a fine step, in the viewport they orbit, and undo, redo, catalogue search, selection cycling and the handle walk each have a chord with English and German labels
  - Entities, handles, check rows and diagrams have accessible roles and names: Given a viewport entity with a utilisation, a handle with limits, a check row and a diagram When their accessibility nodes are built in English and German Then entities are options that speak utilisation and status, handles are sliders with value text and limits, check rows speak status and utilisation, diagrams are images described by their table twin, and every role meets the required attributes of WAI-ARIA 1.2
  - High contrast follows the system: Given forced colours or more contrast When the effective theme is chosen Then the built-in high-contrast theme applies unless the user's bundle is itself high contrast, and its ramps pass the contrast findings
  - A grip speaks its field and its check: Given a handle bound to a length in cm limited to 0 to 10 m and a governing check at 83 % When its slider node is read in English and German Then the name is the field label And the value text is the field value with unit followed by the utilisation And the range is given in the unit of the field
  - Grip changes are announced politely: Given two nudges of one grip When the live region drains Then one polite announcement with the latest value and utilisation is spoken in each language
  - Enter and Escape belong to the focused grip: Given a focused handle When Enter or Escape is pressed Then the value field opens or applies, respectively the change is cancelled
  - Focus on a grip does not rely on colour: Given a focused grip When the grips are drawn Then a cross marks it And forced colours or more contrast draw every grip in the strongest tone And reduced motion changes nothing because grips never animate
  - Control with an arrow key cycles the grips of one entity: Given a focused grip of an entity with several grips When Control with Arrow Right or Arrow Down is pressed Then the next grip of the same entity has the keyboard And Control with Arrow Left or Arrow Up goes back And a plain arrow still nudges the focused grip And the keyboard help lists the chord in English and German
